<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="cardiolipin biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5269" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: CARDIOLIPIN Cardiolipin| is an anionic membrane phospholipid. Along with |FRAME: L-1-PHOSPHATIDYL-GLYCEROL phosphatidylglycerol|, the anionic lipids are important for the biogenesis and function of membranes. Cardiolipin is present in the plasma membrane of bacteria and in the mitochondrial membrane in eukaryotes. A main difference exists between bacterial and eukaryotic cardiolipin biosynthesis; In bacteria cardiolipin is formed by the condensation of two molecules of |FRAME: L-1-PHOSPHATIDYL-GLYCEROL phosphatidylglycerol|, while in eukaryotes it is formed from one molecule of |FRAME: CDPDIACYLGLYCEROL CDP-diacylglycerol| and one molecule of |FRAME: L-1-PHOSPHATIDYL-GLYCEROL phosphatidylglycerol|. In all eukaryotes, cardiolipin biosynthesis occurs in the inner mitochondrial membrane |CITS: [15811335]|. In mung bean mitochondria, the |FRAME: MONOMER-12278| activity is much higher than the |FRAME: MONOMER-12277| activity, resulting in a quick turn over of phosphatidylglycerolphosphate to phosphatidylglycerol |CITS: [12232283]|. In fact, phosphatidylglycerolphosphate is not detected in mitochondrial membranes of mung beans. It is noteworthy that, in addition to being synthesized in mitochondria, phosphatidylglycerol may also be imported from the endoplasmic reticulum to serve as the cardiolipin precursor |CITS: [12609031]|. The reaction catalyzed by |FRAME: MONOMER-12277| is the rate-limiting step of cardiolipin biosynthesis.</Description>
